From b7d2ef36ac0358d574675988163ae5aff4c9cb22 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 07:04:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 066/119] scene-referred S1: the camera's AUTO exposure mode

Fourth item of Stage S1. The exposure the camera publishes can now come from a meter instead of
only from the aperture ring, and the two shipped auto modes finally mean what their names say.

Decision: scene-notes/decisions/s1-auto-mode.md, part 1 approach A (CPU loop on a per-frame
non-blocking readback) and the part 2 meaning table. Research:
scene-notes/research/s1-luminance-measure-and-eye-adaptation.md section 6.

Renderer side, CAutoExposureStage::UpdateSceneReferredMeterReadback(), behind IsSceneReferred():
- Two-phase, non-blocking readback of s_ptexHDRToneMaps[0] (1x1 R16G16F, .y = the geometric-mean
  illuminance estimate with the G-buffer albedo divided out). The texture already carries
  FT_STAGE_READBACK, so it owns a persistent staging resource and neither a copy pass nor a
  texture of our own is needed - the shape is CBloomStage::UpdateDiffractionSourceReadback minus
  its first two steps. Issued every frame rather than once a second, because this closes an
  exposure loop and a one-second cadence would be visible as steps. Cost: four bytes and one
  fence. The blocking one-shot form (what r_HDRDebug 1 uses) is a full pipeline stall and is not
  acceptable per frame; a five-second stall warning says so out loud if the fence never signals.
- Engine passes 1 and 2 keep running untouched. Pass 3 (the temporal adaptation) and both
  ComputeExposure branches are bypassed, NOT stopped: screen-space reflections bind
  m_pTexHDRMeasuredLuminance and the film grain binds s_ptexCurLumTexture, so stopping the stage
  would break them. Only the exposure it produces has stopped being consumed (item 2).
- The pre-exposure is divided out on the renderer side, using the value recorded when the download
  was ISSUED. That is the whole correctness argument of this item: dividing by the current
  exposure instead of the sampled frame's turns the meter into a two-frame-delayed feedback loop
  that oscillates. Doing it here also removes the 2-entry exposure history the decision note asked
  the camera to keep - the stage knows which frame the sample came from, the camera would have had
  to guess. Documented deviation, same result, less machinery.
- Cube-map generation is excluded (it renders at unity and nobody meters it), and leaving the
  scene-referred path retires the reported value so the camera cannot meter off a stale sample.

The channel to the plugin: EFQ_GetSceneReferredMeteredIlluminance, one new ERenderQueryTypes value
and one case in CRenderer::EF_QueryImpl. Deviation from the item's brief, which offered
ICinematicCameraOptics or the post-effect bus: neither is a renderer -> plugin channel. The optics
interface is what CinematicCamera EXPORTS to other plugins (the phone tracker), and CParamFloat's
per-thread slots are synchronised main -> render only, so a render-thread write never reaches the
main thread. EF_Query is CE's own extension point for exactly this and costs two lines.

Camera side:
- AUTO meters with the incident form, ISO 2720 at S = 100 and C = 120*pi - the same constant item 2
  adopted, so manual and auto agree by construction: an 18 % reflector under the metered
  illuminance lands on 0.18 either way. Reflected metering (K = 12.5) is the documented
  alternative and would read 3/4 of a stop differently; the engine's albedo-divided estimate is a
  better meter than a real camera's, and the discrepancy would read as a bug.
- Smoothing is frame-rate independent (1 - exp(-dt/tau)) with the camera's own tau, and snaps
  instantly on a cut - the component clears its validity flag on Activate(), mirroring what
  AutoExposure.cpp:165 does for the engine's adaptation. The smoothing step is guarded by the main
  frame id, because the EV is asked for more than once a frame and the response time must not
  depend on how many callers there are.
- Parameter meanings under the switch, exactly the decision's table, with no CRC, name, enum
  string or default changed: AutoBiased = AUTO with an EV bias of (manual EV100 - Neutral EV), so
  at the shipped defaults it biases by -0.385 stops and stays nearly transparent as it always did;
  AutoCompensation = AUTO with exposure compensation only; ExposureResponse = the metering
  response, the fraction of a measured change the camera follows per tau (it slows the approach,
  it does not leave a steady-state error, so a level authored at 0.6 still behaves like a camera
  that does not chase every flicker); NeutralEV = AUTO's reference EV for the bias; Manual is
  still the pure triangle and now says once that Response and NeutralEV drive the auto modes
  instead. Off the switch every one of them behaves exactly as before.
- Four genuinely new controls, new CRCs, Schematyc names [A-Za-z0-9_]+: Auto Exposure Lock
  (freeze the metered EV, AE lock), Auto Exposure Response Time (tau, default 1 s), Auto Exposure
  EV Min / EV Max (defaults -2 and 20, applied after the bias). The EV limits are the operator's,
  not the time of day's, and they are what stops AUTO walking off the end of a scene lit with real
  light ratios.
- Before the first measurement, and while the meter is stalled, AUTO exposes as Manual rather than
  at EV 0 - which would be a white frame.

Built clean (0 errors, 0 warnings outside Code/SDKs): CryRenderD3D11, CinematicCamera.

